    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d523">

                <pb facs="0602" n="490" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg490"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/829: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American member of the Inter-Allied Railway
                        Committee</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State, transmitted by the Consul at Vladivostok</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caldwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vladivostok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-05-03T17:00:00+08:47"><hi rendition="#italic">May 3, 1919, 5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 4, 4:54 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>273. [From Smith:]</p>
                <p>May 2d Committee unanimously passed resolution that only railway representatives
                    Technical Board have right to hold trains except where no representatives
                    present. Others may hold train if conditions ahead dangerous but Technical Board
                    must immediately be notified. General
                        Khorvat agreed. Resolution passed because General
                        Ivanoff-Rinoff has been searching trains claiming
                    contents may be Bolsheviki munitions.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
